WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
REGRET:
Elon already regrets buying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:07.620
Twitter and it’s not surprising why. According&nbsp;
Elon himself, the current market value of Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:17.220
is less than half of the $44 billion that he paid&nbsp;
to buy the company. This means that he has lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:23.160
over $22 billion with Twitter before even owning&nbsp;
it for a single year. That’s the same as losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:28.800
$2.4 billion every single month since the purchase&nbsp;
and that’s just the loss in market value. Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.960
itself was already burning money when Elon took&nbsp;
over, so it’s likely that Elon has lost even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.640
money running the company as well. Zooming out,&nbsp;
this really doesn’t matter for someone like Elon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.560
Musk given that he’s still up nearly a hundred&nbsp;
billion dollars year to date thanks to Tesla and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:49.680
SpaceX but despite this, it’s not surprising that&nbsp;
Elon still feels like he got the short end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:54.480
stick. In fact, he’s so salty about the deal that&nbsp;
he’s trying to sue the Twitter lawyers who forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.620
him to complete the deal. You see, when Elon&nbsp;
Musk started trying to back out of the Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.480
deal an year ago, Twitter went out and found the&nbsp;
best lawyers they could which turned out to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.240
firm called Wachtell, Lipton, Rosen &amp; Katz. As&nbsp;
we now know, this law fim was indeed successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:14.580
at getting Elon to follow through with his offer&nbsp;
but in return they charged Twitter $90 million for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.500
essentially 4-5 months of work. Twitter’s own&nbsp;
staff itself were stunned when they saw this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.720
fee. In fact, a Twitter director named Martha&nbsp;
Lane Fox would email the general counsel quote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:31.020
“O My Freaking God” after seeing the fee. But,&nbsp;
while the fee was indeed exhorbitant, this didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.280
really matter for Twitter’s original executive&nbsp;
team because, they weren’t the ones paying the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.600
bill. This bill didn’t come in until after Elon&nbsp;
had agreed to buy Twitter again, so ironically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.400
it was Elon who had to pay the lawyers that&nbsp;
forced him into the deal. It’s no wonder why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.080
he’s so butt hurt but does Elon actually have a&nbsp;
chance at winning this case and recouping his $90&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:54.540
million or is Elon just about to burn even more&nbsp;
money on legal fees trying to win this lawsuit.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:02:00.420
THE CASE FOR ELON:
The case for Elon is pretty straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.800
forward. It breaks down into two parts with the&nbsp;
first part centering around Twitter’s executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.240
team. Essentially, he’s arguing Twitter’s&nbsp;
executives did nothing about the ridiculous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:15.180
bill because well they weren’t the ones paying the&nbsp;
bill. So, they weren’t exactly motivated to act in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:20.520
Twitter or Elon’s best interest. In fact, paying&nbsp;
these lawyers $90 million was just one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.740
executives’ many lavish expenses as they headed&nbsp;
out. So, in that context, all of Twitter’s actions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:30.240
before Elon’s takeover should be questioned&nbsp;
because the people in charge simply didn’t care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.040
about the final outcome more than just winning the&nbsp;
lawsuit. They were essentially lame ducks who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.000
collecting their last paychecks before they were&nbsp;
gonna get fired. When you really think about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.740
by fighting Elon Musk and forcing him to close,&nbsp;
these executives were literally putting themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:49.080
out of really good job. So, it’s safe to assume&nbsp;
that they would not only be unmotivated to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.880
in Elon's best interest but that they were&nbsp;
actively resentful against Elon. Don’t forget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:59.400
Twitter didn’t even want to sell themselves to&nbsp;
Elon. In fact, they were actively trying to avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.900
getting acquired by using things like the poison&nbsp;
pill. The only reason they eventually agreed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.880
the acquisition is because Elon’s offer was just&nbsp;
so stupidly high that they would open themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.500
up to shareholder lawsuits if they didn’t take&nbsp;
the deal. And now Elon was trying to get a lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.280
price by threatening to back out of the deal&nbsp;
and they were the ones who had to fight for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.680
Elon to close? Yeah, it would more surprising if&nbsp;
these executives weren’t pissed off. So, Elon is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:27.360
attempting to argue that the actions of these&nbsp;
executives were not only reckless and actively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.520
not in Twitter’s best interest. The second part&nbsp;
of Elon’s argument claims that Wachtell leveraged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:36.600
this situation to get away with ludicrous&nbsp;
pricing. There’s really no question about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:41.880
this. In terms of hourly billing itself, Elon&nbsp;
claims that the Wachtell charged Twitter $15.6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:47.220
million. Many of these billing invoices didn’t&nbsp;
even have proper time entry descriptions. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:52.380
they were completely blank. Yet, Twitter paid them&nbsp;
anyway. The likely reason for this was that simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:57.840
that the hours were probabaly unreasonable even&nbsp;
with a massive hourly fee. Wachtell pulls in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.660
whopping $3.86 million in revenue per lawyer per&nbsp;
year. Given that the average lawyer bills around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:08.940
1900 hours per year, we’re looking at an hourly&nbsp;
fee of about $2000. Twitter likely went for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:14.340
best lawyers at the firm, so their hourly fee was&nbsp;
likely a higher but they did get a 15% discount,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:20.640
so we’ll just assume an hourly fee of $2000. This&nbsp;
means that to rack up $15.6 million dollars worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:26.280
of hourly fees, Wachtell charged twitter for&nbsp;
roughly 7800 hours of work all within a matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:31.380
of 18 weeks. In other words, Wachtell is basically&nbsp;
claiming that they were consistently putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:36.840
in over 400 hours of work every week to defend&nbsp;
Twitter. I don’t know about you but Elon feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:42.720
that this itself is completely unjustifiable.&nbsp;
But that doesn’t even encompass the vast majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:48.360
of the $90 million fee. You see, most of the $90&nbsp;
million was charged to Twitter after Elon agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:53.220
to buy Twitter as a sort of “success fee”. The&nbsp;
only problem with this success fee was that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.780
was never mentioned in the original deal and it&nbsp;
seems that Twitter’s executives were well aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:03.480
of this considering their reaction. But despite&nbsp;
this, paying the invoice was literally the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.940
thing they did presumably in a screw you type of&nbsp;
manner. In fact, the wire payment was completed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:13.980
just 10 minutes before Elon closed the deal and&nbsp;
the executives were fired. So, the evidence does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:18.900
suggest that there may have been some bad faith&nbsp;
from the Twitter team. And this is what Elon is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:22.860
really trying to hammer in on. Wachtell was&nbsp;
over here handing out invoices that didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:27.780
even have proper hours and charging unagreed upon&nbsp;
success fees in the tens of millions. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:32.220
Twitter didn’t even question a single invoice. In&nbsp;
fact, they made sure that they were paid before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:37.200
Elon tookover but this doesn’t mean that&nbsp;
Elon has a victory in the bag by any means.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:42.660
THE CASE AGAINST ELON:
For starters, Twitter’s executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:47.340
aren’t required to have any sort of loyalty&nbsp;
to Elon whatsoever. They worked for the public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:52.440
shareholders and their only job was to maximize&nbsp;
profit for shareholders. From this perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:58.080
they did exactly that even in situations when&nbsp;
they didn’t want to. They didn’t want to agree to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.640
Elons takeover offer in the first place but they&nbsp;
did because that was in the shareholders’ best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.620
interest. Similarly, when Elon wanted to back out&nbsp;
of the deal, Twitter’s executives probably didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:12.180
want to fight him but they did because that was in&nbsp;
the shareholders’ best interest. And at the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:16.740
the day, Twitter’s executives not only made sure&nbsp;
that the acquisition was completed but that it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:21.120
completed for the stupidly high price the Elon&nbsp;
originally offered. So, from that perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:25.200
Twitter’s executives never came short on&nbsp;
their duties to the shareholders. The only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.880
party that they arguably came up short with is&nbsp;
Elon, someone they have no contractual obligation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:35.160
to act in the best interest of. And as for the&nbsp;
law firm, though their fees are indeed ridiculous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:39.900
you already know how they’re gonna argue their&nbsp;
case. They’re not gonna try to defend the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:44.700
that they were actually working for over 400&nbsp;
hours every week. They’re gonna argue that it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:49.140
not about how much time they spent but rather&nbsp;
how much value they bring. Around the time that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:54.480
Wachtell got involved, Twitter was hovering around&nbsp;
a market cap of $30 billion or $14 billion less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.640
than what Elon had agreed to pay. This means that&nbsp;
by getting involved, Wachtell was able to add $14&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:06.780
billion worth of value to shareholders for which a&nbsp;
$90 million fee is miniscule. It’s only 0.6%. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:11.040
Elon could of course argue that they didn’t&nbsp;
have to do much to actually close the deal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.020
Elon had agreed to a specific performance clause&nbsp;
meaning that if Twitter met certain thresholds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:21.780
Elon had to go through with the acquisition. In&nbsp;
other words, he couldn’t just back out and pay a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:26.760
penalty. So, really Wachtell didn’t even have to&nbsp;
convince Elon to go through with the deal. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:31.620
they had to do was disprove Elon’s little fake&nbsp;
bot argument and prove that Twitter did indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:37.260
meet the thresholds outlined in the original&nbsp;
offer. Obviously light work for $90 million but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:42.780
that’s not Wachtell’s fault. That’s Elon’s fault&nbsp;
for one, signing a deal that was hard to back out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:47.820
of and then two, threatening to back out anyway&nbsp;
without a strong case. Wachtell has nothing to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:52.140
with any of that. As far as they’re concerned,&nbsp;
their job is to help shareholders realize $14&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:58.140
billion in lost value and that’s exactly what they&nbsp;
did. Again, their job is not to act in Elon’s best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:03.120
interest. Moreover, it’s not like Elon is suing&nbsp;
another billionaire or another company who may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.940
have made some missteps along the way. They’re&nbsp;
literally suing a straight up law firm. Common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:15.000
sense would tell you that sure a law firm might&nbsp;
do things that are shady or even unjust but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:20.250
can be sure as hell that it’s legal. Here’s the&nbsp;
thing, this isn’t Wachtell’s first rodeo. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:24.660
specialize in mergers and acquisitions and&nbsp;
naturally one party or the other gets mad at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:30.060
them on a regular basis like Carl Icahn did back&nbsp;
in 2012. If you’re not familiar with Carl Icahn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:34.920
he’s one of the most infamous corporate raiders of&nbsp;
all time. And during one of his corporate raids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:39.120
he realized that one of the companies that he&nbsp;
was raiding was using none other than Wachtell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.760
And guess what, they were charging a success fee.&nbsp;
If Icahn’s raid succeeded, the company was on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:51.480
hook for $36 million. If it failed, they only had&nbsp;
to pay $18 million. Icahn was obviously outraged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:56.160
after seeing this and decided to take Wachtell&nbsp;
to court but guess what happened. The case would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:02.160
just end up dragging on for 6 years after which&nbsp;
Icahn would end up losing. So, if history is any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:07.620
indication of how suing over success fees ends&nbsp;
up, well it’s not looking too great for Elon.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:13.740
THE FATE OF TWITTER:
As you can see, Elon has a decent case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:19.020
from a what’s fair type of perspective. There’s&nbsp;
no question that Wachtell charged Twitter as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:24.540
as they possibly could and Twitter was more than&nbsp;
happy to be these fees just to spite Elon. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:29.340
it seems fair that Elon should get a refund&nbsp;
on much of these legal fees. But see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:34.620
what’s fair and what’s legal aren’t always the&nbsp;
same thing. Sure, when you look at it from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:39.720
perspective of, Wachtell got paid $90 million&nbsp;
just to disprove Elon’s little bot claims and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:44.700
finish up the acquisition, it’s a stupid amount.&nbsp;
But, when you look at it from the perspective of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:48.180
Wachtell ensured shareholders got the $14&nbsp;
billion worth of cash that they were promised,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:53.700
$90 million isn’t all that crazy. Similarly, you&nbsp;
can blame the Twitter executives all you want for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.700 --> 00:10:00.180
acting in spite as they probably did. But, they&nbsp;
only acted in spite against Elon not against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:05.640
shareholders which is who they’re supposed to be&nbsp;
loyal to. Also, think about this: Elon would’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:10.380
never been in this position in the first place if&nbsp;
he didn’t try to back out of the deal. If he truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:14.520
was worried about the Twitter bot population,&nbsp;
he should’ve cleared up those concerns before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:20.040
signing a $44 billion deal to buy the entire&nbsp;
company. Considering all of this and the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:24.360
that Wachtell has already successfully defended&nbsp;
themselves in a similar situation against another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:30.540
rich billionaire, it stands to reason that Elon&nbsp;
probably wont be getting a refund and even if he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:35.220
does, it won’t be anytime soon and it won’t really&nbsp;
make a difference to Twitter’s survivability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:40.140
So, maybe it would be a better idea to&nbsp;
focus on turning around Twitter instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:45.840
of wasting time on a lawsuit but that’s&nbsp;
just what I think. Speaking of Carl Icahn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.820
he’s actually facing some massive ponzi scheme&nbsp;
allegations. Check out this video to learn more.

